SUMMARY:The Cat Returns
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260803T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260803T150000
DTSTAMP:20260611T093549Z
UID:019dd517-16d7-e8e7-5dcb-d39a9c5fd257@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, August 3\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museu
 m of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 15m\n\n2002 | 75 min. | Japan | Color 
 | Japanese with English subtitles | Rated G | DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Hiroyuki M
 orita \nWRITTEN BY: Reiko Yoshida \nWITH: Chizuru Ikewaki\, Yoshihiko Ha
 kamada\, Aki Maeda\, Takayuki Yamada\n\nType: Screenings\n\nA spin-off of 
 Whisper of the Heart\, The Cat Returns finds another schoolgirl encounteri
 ng a mysterious tomcat on the street\, though this time she is led to a fa
 ntastic world beyond her wildest dreams. Launched into a cobblestoned magi
 c kingdom ruled by cat royalty—including a motorcade replete with vassal
 s\, maidens\, and even Secret Service—she must enlist the help of the el
 usive Baron Humbert von Gikkingen (the same cat featured as a statue in Wh
 isper of the Heart) if she hopes to make it back home. The only feature fi
 lm directed by Hiroyuki Morita to date\, The Cat Returns is a feline fligh
 t of fancy.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-cat-returns-019dd
 517-16d7-e8e7-5dcb-d39a9c5fd257
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:A Man Escaped in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260803T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260803T223000
DTSTAMP:20260611T093549Z
UID:019dd584-92b4-fd24-11c8-7d0ce23af494@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, August 3\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museu
 m of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 41m\n\n1956 | 101 min. | France | Blac
 k-and-White | French\, German | Not Rated | 35mm  \nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: 
 Robert Bresson \nWITH: François Leterrier\, Charles Le Clainche\, Mauric
 e Beerblock\, Roland Monod\n\nType: Screenings\n\n“On one side\, thick w
 alls\, bars\, soldiers\, weapons. On the other\, one lone man\, unarmed\, 
 doomed from the start.”\nIn minimalist Robert Bresson’s 1956 film\, a 
 French Resistance fighter attempts escape from a German military prison ag
 ainst impossible odds. While the title of the film seems to say it all\, i
 t belies the film’s gripping tension and near-silent sequences of harrow
 ing engineering feats\, unbelievable patience\, and human resilience\, all
  performed with placid determination by François Leterrier as he embodies
  lieutenant Fontaine\, a proxy for André Devigny\, whose life Bresson loo
 sely adapted for the screen from his 1956 memoir.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/a-man-escaped-019dd58
 4-92b4-fd24-11c8-7d0ce23af494
END:VEVENT
