BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Basic Instinct in 4K
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260807T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260807T223000
DTSTAMP:20260611T093549Z
UID:019dd587-0e22-9625-5806-8b921623f8a2@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, August 7\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museu
 m of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 7m\n\n1992 | 127 min. | USA | Color | 
 Scope | English | Not Rated | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Paul Verhoeven \nWRIT
 TEN BY: Joe Eszterhas \nWITH: Michael Douglas\, Sharon Stone\, George Dzu
 ndza\, Jeanne Tripplehorn\n\nType: Screenings\n\nHardboiled San Francisco 
 detective Nick Curran (Michael Douglas) is assigned to the case of a murde
 red rock star and gets in over his head when he meets the victim’s lover
 \, Catherine Tramell (Sharon Stone). A crime novelist whose latest work mi
 rrors Curran’s case\, and past relationships with violent people\, Cathe
 rine becomes the prime suspect. Confronted by Tramell’s smoldering sensu
 ality and mysterious past\, Curran struggles to maintain his professionali
 sm and not give into his carnal instincts. \nPaul Verhoeven’s erotic thr
 iller made a huge impact\, serving as a pop-culture reference for many yea
 rs and defining the genre for the 1990s. The recipient of Academy Award no
 minations for editing (Frank J. Urioste) and Jerry Goldsmith’s original 
 score\, Basic Instinct\, originally edited for sexual and violent content\
 , has been restored in 4K and is now available in Verhoeven’s original\,
  uncut version.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/basic-instinct-019dd5
 87-0e22-9625-5806-8b921623f8a2
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Harriet the Spy in 4K
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260808T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260808T121500
DTSTAMP:20260611T093549Z
UID:019db102-fe01-43a3-84a5-eeeb5fb60e21@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, August 8\, 2026 at 11 am\n\nLocation: Academy Museu
 m of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 40m\n\n1996 | 100 min. | USA | Color |
  English | Rated PG | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Bronwen Hughes \nWRITTEN BY: D
 ouglas Petrie\, Theresa Rebeck. \nWITH: Michelle Trachtenberg\, Rosie O’
 Donnell\, Vanessa Lee Chester\, Gregory Smith\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThe f
 irst motion picture produced by Nickelodeon is also Bronwen Hughes’s fea
 ture film directorial debut\, starring Michelle Trachtenberg as a 12-year-
 old Harriet Welsch\, who devotes her time spying on everyone and recording
  her discreet detective work in a “PRIVATE” notebook. When those secre
 ts get exposed\, Harriet finds her entire class against her\, including he
 r two best friends. This sweet drama boldly and candidly surveys the bitte
 rsweet pre-teen years as an exploration of self\, complementing the confus
 ion that marks this transitional phase with Harriet’s unapologetic\, che
 eky spirit\, letting her fight her own battles and attain redemption.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/harriet-the-spy-019db
 102-fe01-43a3-84a5-eeeb5fb60e21
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Popcorn Disabilities Conversation and Book Signing with Kristen Lo
 pez
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260808T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260808T160000
DTSTAMP:20260611T093549Z
UID:019ddf76-e982-c361-1f01-60e1b84b6d8b@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Book Signings,Conversations
DESCRIPTION:Journalist\, author\, and disability advocate Kristen Lopez si
 gns copies of her book\, Popcorn Disabilities: The Highs and Lows of Disab
 led Representation in the Movies.\n\nSaturday\, August 8\, 2026 at 2 pm\n\
 nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nType: Book Signings\, Conv
 ersations\n\nJoin journalist\, author and disability advocate Kristen Lope
 z as she speaks with particular authority on how disability is represented
 —and too often misrepresented—in movies. It’s all examined in her bo
 ok\, Popcorn Disabilities: The Highs and Lows of Disabled Representation i
 n the Movies. \nPopcorn Disabilities is Lopez’s impassioned\, yet fun an
 d engaging survey of how Hollywood has dealt with disability over the last
  century. She reveals that\, even when they're not just narrative props to
  help an able-bodied protagonist\, movie characters with disabilities are 
 overwhelmingly white\, affluent\, and conventionally attractive\, obscurin
 g the variety of disabilities and the experiences of those who deal with t
 hem. But she also explains where films have gotten it right\, and how the 
 power of the medium can enlighten and educate in the future. \nFrom little
 -remembered gems such as Tod Browning's Freaks (1932)—one of the earlies
 t attempts to show dimensionality in characters with disabilities—to con
 temporary films such as CODA (2021)\, My Left Foot (1989)\, A Different Ma
 n (2024)\, and many others\, it challenges popular assumptions about disab
 ility while never losing sight of movies' unique power\, influence\, and p
 otential as a tool for social good.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/popcorn-disabilities-
 book-signing-with-kristen-lopez-019ddf76-e982-c361-1f01-60e1b84b6d8b
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Fritz Lang’s M
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260808T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260808T150000
DTSTAMP:20260611T093549Z
UID:019dd589-21a2-cf22-47aa-909c46d0348a@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: Academy Governor Howard A. Rodman\n\nSaturday\, Aug
 ust 8\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\n
 Runtime: 1h 51m\n\n1931 | 111 min. | Germany | Black and white | German | 
 PG-13 | DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Fritz Lang \nWRITTEN BY: Fritz Lang\, Thea von 
 Harbou \nWITH: Peter Lorre\, Otto Wernickle\, Inge Landgut\, Gustaf Grün
 dgens\n\nType: Screenings\n\nOne of the most influential filmmakers—whos
 e innovative cinematic language led to such groundbreaking films such as M
 etropolis (1927) and Woman in the Moon (1929) —is Austrian-born Fritz La
 ng\, who contributed to the premise of sci-fi and noir film domains in Ame
 rican cinema. Lang’s first sound film\, M\, is set in a 1930s Berlin dis
 rupted by a gruesome serial murder case targeting children. Peter Lorre st
 ars as the creepy murderer\, Hans Beckert\, who embodies the instability a
 nd confusing socio-political shifts that the nation was facing in the earl
 y 1930s. Pursued by the city police and criminals from the underworld\, Be
 ckert’s heightened anxiety is manifested through an array of daring clos
 e-up shots in high-contrast\, stark\, black-and-white cinematography\, an 
 apparent heir of Lang’s early work in German Expressionism.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/fritz-langs-m-019dd58
 9-21a2-cf22-47aa-909c46d0348a
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The 4th Man in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260808T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260808T223000
DTSTAMP:20260611T093549Z
UID:019dd589-d8d0-bf49-4cbf-adea87d13136@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, August 8\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Mus
 eum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 42m\n\n1983 | 102 min. | Netherlands
  | Color | Dutch with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY: P
 aul Verhoeven\nWRITTEN BY: Gerard Soeteman\nWITH: Jeroen Krabbé\, Renée 
 Soutendijk\, Thom Hoffman\, Dolf de Vries\nPrint courtesy of the Constella
 tionCenter Collection at the Academy Film Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\n
 In Paul Verhoeven’s stylish\, psychological-erotic thriller\, author Ger
 ard (Jeroen Krabbé)\, begins a romantic affair with Christine (Renée Sou
 tendijk)\, an enigmatic widow from literary society\, only to become obses
 sed with her lover\, Herman (Thom Hoffman). While continuing the affair in
  hopes of getting closer to Herman\, Gerard is plagued by ominous visions\
 , fueling paranoid thoughts about Christine plotting murder. Based on the 
 novel by celebrated Dutch author Gerard Reve\, The 4th Man was Verhoeven
 ’s final film shot in Dutch before directing English-language and Hollyw
 ood productions. It was the Netherlands’ official submission for Interna
 tional Feature Film at the 56th Academy Awards\, and\, upon its release in
  the US\, gave American audiences a taste of Verhoeven’s unabashed take 
 on sex\, violence\, and religion.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-4th-man-019dd589-
 d8d0-bf49-4cbf-adea87d13136
END:VEVENT
