 mami\, George Tokoro \nNew subtitled print courtesy of the Academy Film A
 rchive\, with special thanks to Studio Ghibli.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nPony
 o focuses on the friendship between 5-year-old Sosuke and a magical goldfi
 sh named Brunhilde\, the young daughter of a sorcerer father and a sea-god
 dess mother. After wandering away from her father’s four-flippered subma
 rine\, Brunhilde washes ashore and is discovered by the curious Sosuke. No
 w renamed Ponyo\, she forms a quick bond with Sosuke that fuels her yearni
 ng to be a real girl\, and together they shoulder the cost of what that mi
 ght take. The vibrant and colorful hand-drawn animations are a delight for
  audiences of all ages\, as Hayao Miyazaki builds a world filled with magi
 cal goldfish\, spellcasters\, and sea goddesses in this tale of how friend
 ships can blossom anywhere—even in the ocean’s depths.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/ponyo-019dd51f-cf25-5
 1cc-c97d-ba287de07ded
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Out of Sight
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260819T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260819T223000
DTSTAMP:20260611T093549Z
UID:019dd532-0b98-b1eb-af29-bc9f09d740b6@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Selected by the Film Editors Branch\n\nWednesday\, August 19\,
  2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime
 : 2h 3m\n\n1998 | 123 min. | USA | Color | English | Rated R | DCP\nDIRECT
 ED BY: Steven Soderbergh \nWRITTEN BY: Scott Frank \nWITH: George Clooney\
 , Jennifer Lopez\, Ving Rhames\, Don Cheadle\n\nType: Screenings\n\nBank r
 obber Jake Foley’s prison escape is complicated by an inconvenient encou
 nter with U.S. marshal Karen Sisco\, and an unexpected attraction between 
 them. Making his first major studio film\, director Steven Soderbergh\, wo
 rking with an Oscar nominated screenplay by Scott Frank\, brings the world
  of master crime novelist Elmore Leonard to the screen in a dazzling combi
 nation of old-fashioned Hollywood glamour and contemporary grit. The film 
 was also nominated for its editing\, by the accomplished Anne V. Coates (L
 awrence of Arabia\, 1962)\, who employs intercutting\, flashbacks\, and fr
 eeze frames to enhance the swooningly romantic chemistry of stars George C
 looney and Jennifer Lopez.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/out-of-sight-019dd532
 -0b98-b1eb-af29-bc9f09d740b6
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Searchers in 70mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260820T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260820T223000
DTSTAMP:20260611T093549Z
UID:019de50c-7daf-6b53-eb10-eaee76152d11@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Thursday\, August 20\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Mu
 seum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 59m\n\n1956 | 119 min. | USA | Colo
 r | English | 70mm \nDIRECTED BY: John Ford \nWRITTEN BY: Frank S. Nugent
  \nWITH: John Wayne\, Jeffrey Hunter\, Vera Miles\, Ward Bond\n\nType: Scr
 eenings\n\nWhen the family of Civil War veteran Ethan Edwards (John Wayne)
  is killed in a Comanche raid\, he spends years looking for his kidnapped 
 niece—but her uncle may prove more of a threat than her abductors. John 
 Ford’s epic\, morally complex Western drama has become one of his most r
 evered films in the 70 years since its release. It has been cited as a fav
 orite by many filmmakers\, including Francis Ford Coppola\, Martin Scorses
 e and Steven Spielberg\, and has influenced films as diverse as Star Wars 
 (1977) and Hardcore (1979). Three-time Oscar winner Winton C. Hoch (The Qu
 iet Man\, 1952) shot the film in VistaVision on stunning outdoor locations
 \, including Monument Valley\, culminating in a powerful\, frequently imit
 ated final image.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-searchers-019de50
 c-7daf-6b53-eb10-eaee76152d11
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Ponyo in 35mm
